## 1. System Overview

{{ project_name }} is a Vehicle Control Unit (VCU) for powertrain domain control
including torque management, motor control, and thermal management.
Built on NXP {{ mcu }} (dual-core) with ASIL {{ asil_label }} decomposition.

---

## 2. Torque Management

### VCU-TRQ-001: Driver Demand Processing
- The system SHALL read accelerator pedal position from two redundant sensors
- The system SHALL validate pedal position by cross-checking both sensor channels
- The system SHALL map validated pedal position to driver torque demand
- The system SHALL detect pedal plausibility faults within 10 ms

### VCU-TRQ-002: Torque Arbitration
- The system SHALL arbitrate torque requests from: driver, cruise control, stability control (ESP)
- The system SHALL apply minimum-request-wins strategy for safety
- The system SHALL limit torque slew rate to 500 Nm/s maximum
- The system SHALL execute torque interventions from ESP within 5 ms

### VCU-TRQ-003: Torque Monitoring
- The system SHALL monitor actual torque vs demanded torque deviation
- The system SHALL detect torque exceedance > 10% of demand for more than 50 ms
- The system SHALL trigger torque reduction path on monitoring failure

---

## 3. Motor Control

### VCU-MOT-001: Inverter Interface
- The system SHALL send torque setpoint to inverter via CAN every 10 ms
- The system SHALL read inverter status (actual torque, speed, temp) every 10 ms
- The system SHALL detect inverter communication timeout > 50 ms

### VCU-MOT-002: Regenerative Braking
- The system SHALL coordinate regen torque with friction brake via ESP
- The system SHALL limit regen torque to battery SOC and temperature limits
- The system SHALL disable regenerative braking during ABS intervention

---

## 4. Pedal Monitoring (ASIL D)

### VCU-PED-001: Redundant Pedal Sensors
- The system SHALL read two independent accelerator pedal position sensors (APP1, APP2)
- The system SHALL use the lower value when sensor deviation > 5%
- The system SHALL detect sensor plausibility fault when deviation > 10%
- The system SHALL enter degraded mode (limp-home) on pedal sensor fault

### VCU-PED-002: Brake Pedal Monitoring
- The system SHALL detect brake pedal application via two independent switches
- The system SHALL request torque reduction when brake is applied with accelerator
- The system SHALL prioritize brake-over-accelerator (BOA) logic

---

## 5. Safety Requirements (ASIL D)

### VCU-SAF-001: ASIL D Decomposition
- The system SHALL decompose ASIL D requirements into ASIL B(D) + ASIL B(D) independent channels
- The system SHALL implement diverse software on the two cores (Lockstep mode)
- The system SHALL perform cross-core comparison of torque command at frame rate

### VCU-SAF-002: Safe Torque Path
- The system SHALL implement two independent torque monitoring paths
- Path 1 (TorqueManager): calculate demanded torque
- Path 2 (TorqueMonitor): independently verify torque demand via lookup table
- The system SHALL activate torque reduction if Path 1 and Path 2 disagree

### VCU-SAF-003: Watchdog with Windowed Supervision
- The system SHALL configure windowed watchdog (100 ms window)
- The system SHALL check alive supervision of torque calculation runnable
- The system SHALL execute independent deadline supervision for motor control

### VCU-SAF-004: End-to-End Protection
- The system SHALL protect torque-related CAN signals with E2E profile 1
- The system SHALL check E2E header CRC and sequence counter on every frame
- The system SHALL apply safe torque (zero torque) on 3 consecutive E2E failures

---

## 6. Acceptance Scenarios

### Scenario: Normal Acceleration
- GIVEN the vehicle is in Drive mode
- WHEN driver applies 50% accelerator pedal
- THEN the torque demand SHALL be calculated within 10 ms
- AND the torque setpoint SHALL be sent to inverter within 10 ms
- AND the actual torque SHALL track demand within 5%

### Scenario: Brake-Over-Accelerator
- GIVEN the vehicle is accelerating with 30% pedal
- WHEN the driver applies brake pedal
- THEN the system SHALL reduce torque demand to 0 within 20 ms
- AND the torque monitoring path SHALL confirm reduction within 50 ms

### Scenario: Pedal Sensor Fault
- GIVEN APP1 reads 50% and APP2 reads 60%
- WHEN deviation exceeds 5% threshold
- THEN the system SHALL use the lower value (50%)
- AND SHALL set a DTC for pedal plausibility warning
